@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — the Dogon knowledge of the companion of Sirius
#
# An origination and not a Dogon claim. What the tradition file holds is what
# the article reports informants as saying: a seed-star, small and heavy,
# running a course around Sirius. What circulates outside is something else —
# that a west African people possessed, before contact, astronomical knowledge
# of an invisible star that European instruments reached only in the
# nineteenth century. The step from the first to the second is made in the
# recording work and not in the transmission, and it is made visibly: in a
# footnote citing a French astronomical periodical.
#
# The examination of the claim stands in examinations/dogon-sirius.ttl.
#

pmc:DogonKnowledgeOfSiriusCompanion a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "The Dogon knew the companion of Sirius"@en, "Die Dogon kannten den Begleiter des Sirius"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a people without instruments held, as transmitted knowledge, the movements and characteristics of a star that cannot be seen: its period around Sirius, its extreme density, and its priority in creation. The claim is put forward in an article of nineteen fifty which sets the question of how such knowledge could exist and expressly declines to answer or even to pose it."@en ;
    skos:note "The decisive sentence is the one the reception never quotes. On p. 274 the recorders write that the question of how men without instruments know the movements and certain characteristics of practically invisible bodies has not been decided, nor even raised. That sentence grants the premise and withholds the enquiry in one move, and everything the claim later became rests on the granted premise."@en ;
    skos:note "The second decisive sentence is a footnote. Where the article gives the orbital period as some fifty years, the note observes that this period is very close to that of the companion of Sirius and cites a French astronomical periodical of nineteen thirty-one for it. The identification is therefore the recorders' and is made in the recorders' own apparatus; the body of the text never says Sirius B. What the informants are reported as saying and what the note makes of it are separable on the page, and separating them is what this node exists for."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as an origination and not as a dispute, because a dispute needs two sides that each say something about the matter. Here one side says nothing: no transmission carries the identification, and the tradition file holds only the seed-star. The identification begins in the work named below."@en ;
    skos:note "The later form of the claim, in which the knowledge is explained by contact with visitors from the Sirius system, begins in a work of nineteen seventy-six that has not been read at the wording in this run and is therefore not entered. See prima-materia#250."@en ;
    pm:originatedBy "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen" ;
    pm:circulatesAs "ancient Dogon astronomy: a pre-contact African tradition holding the existence, period and density of Sirius B, cited both as evidence of a lost indigenous science and as evidence of extraterrestrial contact" ;
    dcterms:date "1950" ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 274, 282 with its first footnote, and 287" .
